```rust
// Setup: N validators with f Byzantine nodes
// Create separate DagState instances for different honest node views

// Step 1: Build common DAG foundation (rounds 0 to R)
// All honest nodes agree on these blocks

// Step 2: Byzantine node creates equivocating blocks at round R+1
// Block_v1: set_commit_votes([vote_for_block_X])
// Block_v2: set_commit_votes([vote_for_block_Y])
// Ensure: same ancestors, different commit_votes, different digests

// Step 3: Selective broadcast
// Add Block_v1 to DagState_group1
// Add Block_v2 to DagState_group2

// Step 4: Build subsequent rounds (R+2 to R+K)
// Each group builds on their respective equivocating block

// Step 5: Trigger commit and linearization
// committer.try_decide() for each view
// linearizer.handle_commit() for each view

// Step 6: Assert safety invariant
// All honest nodes must produce IDENTICAL linearized sequences
// If sequences diverge, SAFETY VIOLATION detected

// Expected: Test should PASS (sequences identical)
// Actual: Test FAILS if protocol has this vulnerability
```

This test pattern can be adapted to other DAG-based consensus protocols by:
- Replacing DagState with the protocol's state management
- Replacing UniversalCommitter with the protocol's commit mechanism
- Replacing Linearizer with the protocol's ordering mechanism
- Adjusting the commit vote structure to match the protocol's voting mechanism
